<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry V, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1489">1489</a>–<a href="/years/1568">1568</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Henry the Younger · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">House of Welf</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry V, Duke of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a>, known as Henry the Younger, ruled the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a> from 1514. A fierce Catholic opponent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, he was driven out by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-league">Schmalkaldic League</a> in 1542 but returned. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-riechenberg">Treaty of Riechenberg</a> of 1552 he took control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rammelsberg">Rammelsberg</a> mine and forests from the city of Goslar.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-v-duke-of-brunswick-luneburg.jpg" alt="Henry V, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg" width="232" height="293" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heinrich_II._(der_J%C3%BCngere)_1489-1568_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Henry was born at Wolfenbüttel Castle on 10 November 1489, the son of Duke Henry IV, called Henry the Elder, and Catherine, daughter of the Griffin duke Eric II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>. His father had received the Principality of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel in a division of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> lands in 1495 and was killed in battle during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> feud in 1514, when Henry succeeded him. He was the last Catholic among the Welf princes, and he became known for the many wars he fought.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He soon entered the Great Diocesan Feud against the Prince-Bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildesheim">Hildesheim</a> and lost the Battle of Soltau in 1519, but his support for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>'s election as emperor paid off, and in 1523 the bishopric had to cede large territories to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a>. He stayed loyal to the emperor in the German Peasants' War and in 1528 fought for him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> against King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-of-france">Francis I of France</a>. Though he first leaned towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a>, he remained Catholic, and in 1541 he attacked the Protestant Imperial City of Goslar, chiefly to gain the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rammelsberg">Rammelsberg</a> mines.</p><p>When Elector John Frederick I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and Landgrave Philip I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> occupied his lands in 1542, he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. An attempt to return in 1545 ended in his capture by Hessian troops, and he was freed and reinstated only after the emperor defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-league">Schmalkaldic League</a> at Mühlberg in 1547. In 1553 he allied with Elector Maurice of Saxony against Margrave Albert Alcibiades of Brandenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulmbach">Kulmbach</a> and won the bloody <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sievershausen">Battle of Sievershausen</a>, in which Maurice and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Henry</a>'s two eldest sons, Charles Victor and Philip, were killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His long affair with Eva von Trott, a lady-in-waiting to his first wife Maria of Württemberg, produced ten children; when rumours spread he staged a mock funeral for her at Gandersheim Abbey while she hid at Liebenburg Castle, and the scandal moved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> to write Wider Hans Worst in 1541. In 1556 he married Sophia Jagiellon, daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-i-the-old">Sigismund I of Poland</a>. Under the influence of his only surviving son, Julius, he finally turned Protestant, and he died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a> on 11 June 1568.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henry V of Brunswick-Lüneburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Treaty of Riechenberg of 1552 it lost control of the mine and forests to <b>Henry the Younger</b> of the Principality of Wolfenbüttel, beginning a long decline.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-goslar">Free Imperial City of Goslar</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Henry the Younger</b> was a fierce Catholic opponent of the Reformation, but his son Julius introduced Lutheranism in 1568 and founded the University of Helmstedt in 1576.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-wolfenbuttel">Principality of Wolfenbüttel</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.16,10.54&amp;year=1540" aria-label="Wolfenbüttel on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4787 362 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Henry V, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5237 632h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5237 632h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.16,10.54&amp;year=1540">What was here in 1540? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
